# E0159: operator on a type that has none

`==` and `!=` need the interface `Eq`, and `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=` need `Ord`. This error is on an operator whose operands have a type that does not have the interface. A type that holds a type parameter `T` can be such a type, when no bound on `T` gives the interface: `<` on `[T]` is this error, because an array has no `Ord`. The type can lack the interface itself, or a part of it can: `[Node]` has no `==` because `Node` has none. The message names that part.

A type has `Eq` when its parts have it: the numbers, `bool`, `char`, `string`, and the arrays, optionals, enums and structs of types that have it. So a struct with an `any` field, or a field that holds a class with no `Eq`, has none. A class has no `Eq` of its own. Only the numbers, `char` and `string` have `Ord`. A class or an enum gets either interface when its program writes the conformance.

## Why

Two names for one class object are the same object, so the test that a class value supports is `a is b`. Comparing the fields of two objects is a choice that a class makes with a written `Eq`, as it chooses its own order with a written `Ord`. A rule of the language that compared the fields would not know the class's own idea of equality.

For a class, the help shows `a is b`. For a value that holds a class, such as `[Node]`, `is` does not apply: make the class conform to `Eq`. When the type has a conformance whose bounds do not hold, as `extend<T: Ord> Pair<T>: Eq` does for a `Pair<bool>`, a note says which bound fails.

A type whose written conformance has a bound that fails gets the help for the part inside it that fails. For a `Pair<Node>` with `extend<A: Eq> Pair<A>: Eq`, the help is `make Node conform to Eq`. The help comes only when each bound that fails needs the same change: with `extend<A: Eq, B: Ord> Two<A, B>: Eq`, a `Two<T, bool>` gets a label on the first bound and no help. A part that no help can fix, such as a `bool` that must conform to `Ord`, gets none.

An operator on a type parameter `T` is [E0139](E0139.md): add a bound to the parameter. So are `==` and `!=` on an array, an optional or an enum that holds a `T`, when its other parts have `Eq`. So are the arithmetic operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and `%`, and a unary `-`, on a type that holds a `T`. No bound gives them. For a comparison, the rule is that a bound on `T` can give it. No bound on `T` gives `<` on a `[T]`, so that is this error. A `contains` on an array whose element type has no `Eq` is [E0154](E0154.md), not this error: the prelude writes `contains` for an element that conforms to `Eq`.

Operands of different types are the mistake, whatever interfaces the types have. That error is [E0047](E0047.md) for `==` and `!=`, and [E0032](E0032.md) for `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and `contains`. An operand that cannot be the type of the other one has the same rule. A number that does not fit is [E0040](E0040.md), [E0042](E0042.md) or [E0104](E0104.md). This error comes when the operands are one type. A `T` beside a `T?` counts as one type. So does an operand whose own error leaves it with no type. An error in an operand that is not about its type, as a wrong `let` in a block, does not hide this error.

An `any` value has no `==` and no `<`: two `any Shape` values may hold values of different types, and a type that the compiler does not know has no equality to ask for. A comparison of an `any` value with a value that converts to it is this error too: `d == c` with `d: any Shape` and `c: Circle` is, as `d == d` is. When the interface inherits `AnyObject`, the help shows `a is b`, which compares the objects that the two values hold. `any Shape` has no such test, because it may hold a number.

## Broken

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
}

fn main() {
    let a = Node(value: 1)
    let b = Node(value: 1)
    print(a == b)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
}

fn main() {
    let a = Node(value: 1)
    let b = Node(value: 1)
    print(a is b)
    print(a.value + b.value)
}
```

